# P1 (SPEC-STANOK-K8S-RUNTIME-2026-10-10 §7) — the network boundary that
# REPLACES the Docker-era stanok-net iptables policy and the bwrap network
# block (O3). The policies select Pods by label, the Jobs carry the labels.
#
# 2.4 (operator 2026-10-10): this manifest carries NO addresses — the three
# egress targets are placeholders rendered from configuration by
# launcher/netpol.py (`python3 -m launcher.netpol` prints the applied
# manifest; infra/netpol-smoke.sh applies exactly what it renders):
#   {{MODEL_IP}}        — the inference server: derived from STANOK_SERVER_URL
#   {{OPIK_HOST_IP}}    — the Opik backend's published host address
#                         (STANOK_OPIK_HOST_IP, default in launcher/config.py)
#   {{OPIK_BACKEND_IP}} — the Opik backend's container address on the compose
#                         network (STANOK_OPIK_BACKEND_IP; pinned in the
#                         compose override — see the CC-225 note below)
# A grep for a dotted IPv4 literal in this file must find nothing: the
# addresses live in configuration, not in the manifest.
#
# PACKAGE 1 (operator audit 2026-10-10):
# * Versioned names: {{REVISION}} is a deterministic hash of the rendered
#   SEMANTIC content (comments and metadata.name excluded — the hash is
#   never self-referential). Cutover is make-before-break: NetworkPolicy
#   rules are ADDITIVE (a Pod's effective permission is the union of every
#   policy selecting it), so applying a stricter policy does NOT revoke what
#   an older policy still allows — the old rules stay effective until the
#   old policy objects are deleted. infra/netpol-smoke.sh applies the new
#   names first, verifies them, and only then deletes the older
#   Stanok-managed names; a DNS blackout is claimed only after the old
#   worker policy is gone.
# * Worker: egress ONLY to the model host (8080) and the Opik backend
#   (8080). NO kube-dns rule (full DNS egress blackout): the worker needs
#   no name resolution — the model and the Opik endpoints are IP literals
#   in the Job env. Everything else is refused: packages from the internet
#   are NOT installable, exactly as in the Docker era.
# * Fresh: default-deny in BOTH directions (policyTypes Ingress + Egress,
#   no rules) — the honest mirror of the Docker fresh container's
#   --network=none. kubectl wait/logs keep working: they ride the
#   kubelet/CRI path, not the Pod network.
#
# CC-225 incident (cc225/cc225-retry1 ConnectionRefused): the netpol
# controller filters Pod egress in filter FORWARD, which sees the POST-DNAT
# destination — a Pod->{{OPIK_HOST_IP}}:8080 packet is DNATed (docker
# published port) to the backend's container address {{OPIK_BACKEND_IP}}
# BEFORE FORWARD, so the {{OPIK_HOST_IP}}/32 rule never matches and the
# packet is REJECTED (ConnectionRefused, not timeout). Ground truth
# (iptables nat dump): the DOCKER chain DNATs the published port to the
# backend's address on the custom compose network, NOT on the docker bridge.
# The post-DNAT destination is therefore allowed by a /32 rule: the
# backend's container address is FIXED in the compose override (ipv4_address,
# outside the dynamic-allocation range), so the policy narrows the whole
# compose network to one /32. The {{OPIK_HOST_IP}}/32 rule is kept as
# documentation of intent (it would match only a non-DNATed path). NOTE: the
# k3s netpol controller ignores NetworkPolicy UPDATES — the versioned names
# make every semantic change a NEW object (apply-then-delete-old), which is
# why the fixed legacy names are retired.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: stanok-worker-egress-{{REVISION}}
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      stanok: worker
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
  egress:
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{MODEL_IP}}/32
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8080
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{OPIK_HOST_IP}}/32
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8080
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{OPIK_BACKEND_IP}}/32
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8080
---
# Fresh (the host's independent verifier Pod): default-deny in BOTH
# directions — policyTypes Ingress + Egress with NO rules. The check must
# not reach the model server or anything else, and nothing may reach it.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: stanok-fresh-deny-all-{{REVISION}}
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      stanok: fresh
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    - Egress
